rred in Jerusalem, the capital of the Kingdom of Judah."}], "places": [{"name": "Jerusalem, Israel", "role": "Major site of prophecy"}, {"name": "Judah, Israel", "role": "Region of ministry"}, {"name": "Israel, Ancient Kingdom", "role": "Context of conflict"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Isaiah", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "isaiah"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q131458", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Isaiah"], "tags": ["religious text", "Other Sacred & Wisdom Texts", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Isaiah left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 105 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Isaiah's prophetic messages reshaped the spiritual landscape of ancient Judah and laid foundational concepts for both Judaism and Christianity, influencing millions over millennia.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-07T13:10:33Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27093366438", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Book of the bible Created by http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/8c5cafb41edf5d7f7c453d396f4165c8.", "newValue": "Isaiah, a major prophetic figure in the Hebrew Bible, thrived during the 8th century BCE, primarily in the Kingdom of Judah. His prophetic ministry occurred amid significant political turmoil marked by the Assyrian threat, which was a crucial period for the ancient Near East. Isaiah's messages addressed both the social injustices of his time and the impending doom that would befall Judah if the people did not return to righteousness. His prophecies are notable for their theological depth and literary beauty, influencing both Jewish and Christian traditions profoundly.

The Book of Isaiah, traditionally attributed to him, is divided into sections that cover themes from judgment to hope, with vivid imagery and poetic language. Key events during his lifetime include the Syro-Ephraimite War (734-732 BCE), when Judah faced invasion from Israel and Aram, and the subsequent Assyrian campaigns. Isaiah famously counseled King Ahaz of Judah against forming alliances with Assyria, advocating instead for trust in God. His prophetic declarations contributed to the shaping of Jewish identity, emphasizing God's sovereignty and the future hope of a messianic age.

